{"id":"scheduling-eviction-workload-aware-preemption","corpus":"k8s","title":"Workload-Aware Preemption","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/workload-aware-preemption/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Workload-Aware Preemption"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the GenericWorkload feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Enable Or Disable Feature Gates for more information."},{"kind":"p","text":"In v1.36, the workload-aware preemption logic was gated by WorkloadAwarePreemption feature gate. This feature gate was merged into GenericWorkload feature gate in v1.37."},{"kind":"p","text":"Workload-aware preemption introduces a preemption mechanism specifically designed for PodGroups. When a PodGroup cannot be scheduled, the scheduler utilizes a preemption logic that tries to make scheduling of this PodGroup possible. This approach is used exclusively during PodGroup scheduling and replaces the default preemption mechanism for pods from a given PodGroup."},{"kind":"p","text":"When this feature is enabled, the scheduler treats the PodGroup as a single preemptor unit, rather than evaluating individual pods from a PodGroup in isolation. To make room for the pending pods in the group, it searches for victims across the entire cluster, and knows how to treat and preempt other PodGroups as victims according to their disruption modes."},{"kind":"p","text":"This feature is coupled with Gang Scheduling and depends on the Workload API. Ensure the [scheduling.k8s.io/v1beta1]API group is enabled in the cluster."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How it works"},{"kind":"p","text":"The workload-aware preemption process follows the same principles as default preemption with a few differences:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Cluster-wide domain: Instead of evaluating preemption node by node, the scheduler evaluates the entire cluster as a single domain. It selects a set of victims across multiple nodes that can be removed to make enough room for the preemptor PodGroup to be scheduled."},{"kind":"li","text":"Victim importance hierarchy: The scheduler decides which preemption units (individual pods or PodGroups) are more critical and should be spared from preemption using a strict hierarchy:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Priority: Higher priority units are always more important."},{"kind":"li","text":"Workload type: PodGroups are considered more important than individual Pods of the same priority."},{"kind":"li","text":"Group size (PodGroups): If both units are PodGroups, the one with more members (larger size) is considered more important."},{"kind":"li","text":"Start time: Units that started earlier are more important."},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod group priority and disruption: The scheduler considers the specific priority and disruption mode of a PodGroup to evaluate if and how its pods can be preempted during preemption events."},{"kind":"li","text":"Performance and optimality considerations: For the performance reasons, the workload-aware preemption first simulates removal all potential victims and runs the scheduling once. It then tries to reprieve as many victims as possible for selected placement. This trade off means that there may exists an alternative placement causing less preemptions, but it is not selected by the scheduler due to performance reasons."},{"kind":"p","text":"When scheduling a single Pod, the default pod preemption applies. In v1.36, when the scheduler performs a default preemption for a single Pod and it attempts to preempt a Pod belonging to a PodGroup, it does not respect the priority or disruptionMode fields of that PodGroup. This limitation no longer applies in v1.37."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Reprieval algorithm"},{"kind":"p","text":"When running workload-aware preemption, the scheduler runs the simulation where it removes potential preemption victims and runs the pod group scheduling algorithm. It then tries to reprieve as many victims as possible for returned placement. To do that, the scheduler reuses the CycleStates from pod group scheduling. For each of the potential victims, sorted by their importance, the scheduler:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Adds victim pods back to their nodes and CycleStates of preemptor pods."},{"kind":"li","text":"For each pod in the PodGroup (in the same order as in scheduling algorithm):"},{"kind":"li","text":"Runs Filter plugins for the pod on its proposed node"},{"kind":"li","text":"Adds the pod to its proposed node"},{"kind":"li","text":"Runs Reserve plugins for the pod on its proposed node"},{"kind":"p","text":"If for each pod, the Filtering passes, the victim pods are kept on their nodes."},{"kind":"p","text":"If filtering fails for at least one pod, victim pods are removed from CycleStates and node."},{"kind":"p","text":"In both cases the scheduler preemptor pods are removed from their nodes and their Unreserve is called, so the next reprieval attempt can validate scheduling of PodGroup. The scheduler then proceeds with another potential victim until all victims are processed."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Preemption for CompositePodGroups"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, you (or a cluster administrator) will need to enable the CompositePodGroup feature gate for all relevant components in your cluster."}],"lemmas":{"workloads":["workload","workloads"],"awares":["awares","aware"],"features":["feature","features"],"featuring":["featuring","feature"],"featured":["featured","feature"],"uses":["use","uses"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"administrators":["administrator","administrators"],"wills":["will","wills"],"will